.\"                                      Hey, vim: ft=nroff
.TH SCREENATOR 1 "October 29, 2026"
.SH NAME
Screenator \- vibe-coded screen annotation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B screenator
.RI [ options ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBScreenator\fP lets you draw annotations over all your running applications.
It can run in the background and be activated on demand. The drawing stays on
screen as long as you want, and you can continue to use your applications
while it is visible.
.br
\fBScreenator\fP is a fork of Gromit-MPX 1.4.2. It adds a small draggable
on-screen icon whose left/right click opens a menu with the drawing tools
(pen, marker, shapes, text and eraser) and the settings (line colour,
thickness, eraser size, opacity, shape fill, text font and floating icon
size). The same menu is available from the system tray icon.
.br
A small settings indicator sits to the left of the floating icon: a rectangle
as tall as the icon, divided into three equal-height pieces centred
horizontally. The upper piece is the current line thickness in the line
colour, the middle piece is 24 pixels of the line colour and the lower piece is
24 pixels of the shape fill colour (a black and white checkerboard when shapes
are not filled). Each piece is surrounded by a one pixel black outline. "Show
Settings Indicator" in the menu turns it off and on; it is on by default and
is disabled while the floating icon is hidden.
.br
The Marker draws a yellow highlighter stroke with a fixed 32 pixel width and
50% opacity. Its settings are independent of the line colour, thickness and
opacity chosen for the other tools.
.br
While painting mode is active, the mouse wheel steps through the line colours;
with the Paint Bucket tool it steps through the shape fill colours and with the
Eraser it steps through the eraser sizes instead. The change is the same as
picking it from the menu, so it is kept when another tool is selected and is
shown in the settings indicator and the menus.
.br
The Shapes submenu contains the Marker, Line, Spline, Polygon, Triangle,
Arrow, Ellipse, Rounded Rectangle, Paint Bucket, Math Function and Parallel
Lines tools.
Shapes are drawn by dragging from the start point to the end point. The line
and the arrow follow the drag direction, which snaps to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 and
so on while Ctrl is held; without it the direction follows the pointer. The
arrow adds a triangular head at the end point that stays easy to see even with
a thin line. A placed line or arrow stays editable:
each end point has a round handle, and dragging the line itself moves the whole
shape; clicking outside, double-clicking or right-clicking commits it.
Ellipses, rounded rectangles, polygons and triangles can be filled using the
"Shapes Fill Color" submenu; the default is no fill and green is its first
colour.
.br
The "Screen Paper" submenu turns the screen into paper for drawing and
writing: None shows the normal desktop, Plain a white page, Ruled a white page
with blue writing lines and Graph a white page with a grey square grid. The
paper is drawn below the annotations and above the other windows, the floating
icon and its menu stay above it, it takes the mouse and keyboard so clicks and
typing do not reach the desktop or the applications below, and it stays in
place when the screen is cleared.
.br
The Spline tool is placed click by click instead: every click adds a node, the
nodes are joined by a smooth Bezier curve, and dragging a node moves it. Every
node has two round Bezier handles; dragging one mirrors the other so the curve
stays smooth, and holding Ctrl while dragging moves only that side, which gives
the node a corner. Click the last node again, double-click or right-click to
finish the curve.
.br
The Polygon tool places a shape with a single click and keeps it editable with
four handles: the square handle controls the number of sides from 3 to 16
(pressing it resets the shape to 3 sides; moving away from it in any direction
adds sides and moving back towards it removes them, down to 3 at the handle),
one round handle sets the radius, one sets the rotation, and one moves the
inner vertices outwards until the shape becomes a regular polygon; that handle
snaps to the flat edge when it gets close, so a regular polygon is easy to
hit. Drag inside the shape to move it. Clicking empty space commits it and
places the next one; double-click or right-click commits it.
.br
The Triangle tool places an equilateral triangle with a single click and keeps
it editable with three round handles, one on every vertex. Dragging a handle
moves that vertex, so the triangle can be stretched and skewed freely; holding
Ctrl while dragging a vertex snaps the angle at that vertex to 30, 45, 60 or
90 degrees. Drag inside the triangle to move the whole shape. Clicking empty
space commits it and places the next one, and a double-click or right-click
commits it.
.br
The Ellipse and Rounded Rectangle stay editable after they have been dragged
out. Every placed shape keeps a square size handle on its bottom-right corner
and a round rotation handle above it, and dragging inside the shape moves it;
rotation is free, and holding Ctrl snaps it to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 and so on.
The Rounded Rectangle also keeps a roundness handle on its top edge:
dragging it away from the corner rounds the corners more, and dragging it
towards the corner makes them sharper. Clicking outside the shape,
double-clicking or right-clicking commits it.
.br
The Math Function tool opens a dialog for the expression and the range. The
Rectangular mode plots y = f(x) over an x range and takes a function of x; the
Polar mode plots r = f(theta) over a theta range, where theta is reserved for
the angle and the default range is 0 to 2*pi, a full circle. "pi" and "e" are
the constants pi and Euler's number, and multiplication can be implied
("2pi*x"). A preset drop-down fills the expression with "cos(2*pi*x)",
"sin(2*pi*x)", "unit(x)", "delta(x)", "e^(x)", "log(x)", "1/x",
"sin(2*pi*x)/(2*pi*x)" or "(cos(2*pi*x)+2)*cos(20*pi*x)"; in polar mode the
same presets use theta instead of x. unit(x) is
the unit step, and delta(x) is the Dirac delta, drawn as an arrow at the point
where its argument is zero. The defaults are "cos(2*pi*x)" from x = -2 to
x = 2 and "cos(3*theta)" from theta = 0 to 2*pi; each mode keeps its own
expression and range. The plot is drawn inside a frame that is dragged out
like the other shapes and is scaled to fill it, so the size handle also sets
its amplitude; polar plots use one scale for both axes, so the curve stays
round in a non-square frame. The plot can be moved, resized and rotated with the usual
handles. Selecting the tool from the Shapes menu asks for the expression
first, and choosing it again changes the expression; a plot that is still
editable updates at once. A point where the
expression is undefined is filled in with its limit when both sides agree, so
"sin(2*pi*x)/(2*pi*x)" is drawn through x = 0; a real pole such as "1/x" or "tan(x)"
breaks the curve instead.
.br
The Parallel Lines tool draws between 2 and 40 evenly spaced vertical lines
with the current line thickness. The square handle outside the left edge sets
their number: pressing it resets the shape to two lines and moving away adds
more. The lines can be moved, resized and rotated with the usual handles.
.br
The Paint Bucket fills the closed area under the click with the Shapes Fill
Color. The area must be enclosed by drawn strokes and must not reach the screen
edge; otherwise nothing is filled, so clicking outside a shape cannot flood the
whole screen. With None selected the bucket erases the enclosed fill instead,
leaving the strokes around it. The tool shows a bucket cursor.
.br
The eraser size can be set to 20, 40, 60 or 120 pixels from the menu. The
erasing area and the circular eraser cursor both match the selected size.
.br
The Text tool lets you write directly on the screen. Click where the text
should start and type: a small borderless editor appears at that position,
using the pen colour and the font selected by "Change Font". The text is
centred vertically on the click point. The text and its blinking caret are
drawn above the drawings and the screen paper, so they stay visible over them.
The middle mouse button still erases while it is held, also with the Text tool
selected. Press Enter to place the text or Escape to cancel it. A placed text
stays editable: drag it to
move it, drag its square handle to change the font size, and drag its round
handle to rotate it.
.br
The pen, font and screen paper settings are remembered in
.I ~/.config/screenator.ini
and can be restored to their defaults with the "Reset Settings" menu entry.
.br
Selecting a tool shows a short hint that describes that tool: its mouse
buttons, what the mouse wheel changes and how its handles work. Each tool's
message can be turned off separately with "Never show this message again";
the Spline hint, which also explains how to move its handles, has its own
option, and the tools whose handles snap show a hint about holding Ctrl to
snap to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 degrees and so on. Reset Settings leaves all the
choices untouched.
.br
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROL
By default, Screenator grabs the "F9" key (this can be changed using the
"\-\-key" option), making it unavailable to other applications. The
available shortcuts are:
.TP
.B F9
toggle painting
.TP
.B SHIFT-F9
clear screen
.TP
.B CTRL-F9
toggle visibility
.TP
.B ALT-F9
quit Screenator
.TP
.B F8
undo last stroke
.TP
.B SHIFT-F8
redo last undone stroke
.PP
While painting mode is active, the usual
.B Ctrl+Z
and
.B Ctrl+Shift+Z
shortcuts also undo and redo. The keys are only grabbed while painting, so
they keep their normal meaning in other applications.
.SH OPTIONS (STARTUP)
A short summary of the available command line arguments for starting Screenator;
see below for the options to control an already running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-active
start Screenator and immediately activate it.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
gives some debug output.
.TP
.B \-k <keysym>, \-\-key <keysym>
change the key used to grab the mouse. <keysym> can e.g. be "F9", "F12",
"Control_R" or "Print". To determine the keysym for different keys you can use
the \fBxev\fP(1) command. You can specify "none" to prevent Screenator from
grabbing a key.
.TP
.B \-K <keycode>, \-\-keycode <keycode>
change the key used to grab the mouse by keycode instead of keysym.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-opacity <value>
set the initial drawing opacity using a value between 0 and 1.
.TP
.B \-u <keysym>, \-\-undo\-key <keysym>
change the key used to undo/redo strokes.
.TP
.B \-U <keycode>, \-\-undo\-keycode <keycode>
change the undo/redo key by keycode instead of keysym.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
show the Screenator version.
.SH OPTIONS (CONTROL)
A short summary of the available command line arguments to control an already
running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clear
clear the screen.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quit
cause the running Screenator process to quit.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-toggle
toggle the grabbing of the pointer.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-visibility
toggle the visibility of the drawing.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-reload
reload the tool configuration.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-redo
redo the last undone drawing stroke.
.TP
.B \-z, \-\-undo
undo the last drawing stroke.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
Screenator uses this to determine which desktop environment it is running on.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_SESSION
Screenator uses this to determine whether it is running under X11 or Wayland.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory to search for the user's configuration files, defaults to
.BI ~ /.config/ .
.TP
.B GDK_CORE_DEVICE_EVENTS
If set, GDK does not use the XInput extension and only reacts to core X input
events, which renders Screenator unusable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator.cfg
User tool configuration: defines pens and maps mouse buttons and modifiers to
them.
.TP
.I /etc/screenator/screenator.cfg
System-wide tool configuration, used when the user configuration does not
exist.
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator.ini
Saved menu settings (line colour, thickness, opacity, shape fill, screen
paper, floating icon size, settings indicator visibility, text font, math
expression and range, and the selected tool).
.SH NOTES
Screenator is X11-only. Under Wayland it runs through XWayland.
